    "body": "### What\n\nThe Megatron weight/optimizer residency adapter and the HF export path called\n`torch.cuda.*` memory APIs directly instead of the repo's platform abstraction.\nThis PR replaces them with `current_platform.synchronize()` /\n`current_platform.clear_memory()` — the same calls the rest of the engine layer\nalready uses (`areal/engine/megatron_engine.py`, `areal/engine/fsdp_engine.py`).\n\nFixes #1786.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nOn a non-CUDA accelerator (Ascend NPU, using the existing `areal/infra/platforms/npu.py`)\nthe hardcoded calls do not work:\n\n| call | on Ascend 910B4 |\n| --- | --- |\n| `torch.cuda.synchronize()` | raises `AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled` |\n| `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` | returns quietly, does **not** release the NPU allocator cache |\n\nso `release_memory()` / `resume_memory()` / `release_grad_memory()` /\n`_offload_*()` abort whenever the Megatron backend is used on NPU, and the\nreclamation calls in `MegatronEngine.save()` and `hf_save` never free device memory.\n\n### Changed\n\n* `areal/engine/megatron_utils/weight_residency.py` — 8 `torch.cuda.synchronize()` →\n  `current_platform.synchronize()`; the two `synchronize()+gc.collect()+empty_cache()`\n  trios → `current_platform.clear_memory()` (which is exactly that trio);\n  the now-unused `gc` import is dropped.\n* `areal/engine/megatron_engine.py` — `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` in `save()` →\n  `current_platform.clear_memory()`.\n* `areal/models/mcore/hf_save.py` — 2 × `torch.cuda.empty_cache()` (state-dict sharding\n  and the pre-ep_pp-gather reclamation) → `current_platform.clear_memory()`.\n* `tests/test_megatron_weight_residency_platform.py` — new regression test asserting\n  `release_memory()`/`resume_memory()` go through the platform object.\n\nDiff: 13 changed lines in production code, no signature or config change.\n\n### Risk\n\nLow on CUDA: `CudaPlatform.clear_memory()` is literally\n`torch.cuda.synchronize(); gc.collect(); torch.cuda.empty_cache()`, i.e. the trio it\nreplaces, and `CudaPlatform`/`NPUPlatform` both already expose `synchronize()`.\nNo behaviour change for CUDA/ROCm users; the only functional difference is on\naccelerators whose `torch.cuda` namespace is a stub.\n\n### Verification (Ascend 910B4, real hardware)\n\nEnv: `torch 2.15.0.dev20260917+cpu`, `torch_npu 2.15.0.dev20260917+gitec69335`,\n`Ascend910B4`, platform resolves to `NPUPlatform` (`current_platform.device_type == \"npu\"`).\n\nHarness drives the real `areal/engine/megatron_utils/weight_residency.py` on the device\nwith a stub engine (so the offload bodies short-circuit) and fake MCore DDP buffers\n(with a `megatron.core.distributed.DistributedDataParallel` shim, since the container\nhas no megatron-core install). Same harness, same file, only the module under test\nswapped — md5 of the module before: `68c75f78fbb749f216d33725cbc8079e`.\n\nBefore:\n\n```\n[path] release_memory()         RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n[path] release_grad_memory()    RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n[path] _offload_model_weights() RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n[path] offload_then_reload()    RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n[path] _offload_optimizer_states() RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n[path] _reload_optimizer_states() RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n[path] resume_memory()          RAISED AssertionError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n```\n\nAfter (module md5 `6a51069537f16e615df704cb2eb10f7d`):\n\n```\n[path] release_memory()            OK\n[path] release_grad_memory()       OK\n[path] ensure_grad_buffers()       OK\n[path] _offload_model_weights()    OK\n[path] offload_then_reload()       OK\n[path] _offload_optimizer_states() OK\n[path] _reload_optimizer_states()  OK\n[path] resume_memory()             OK\n```\n\nMemory reclamation (64 MiB alloc/free cycle on the NPU, same process):\n\n```\n[mem] reserved after alloc/free               = 71303168\n[mem] after torch.cuda.empty_cache()          = 71303168   # unchanged\n[mem] after current_platform.clear_memory()   = 2097152    # released\n```\n\nTest command:\n\n```\npytest tests/test_megatron_weight_residency_platform.py\n```\n\nBoth cases pass. I could not run `uv run pytest` as-is on the NPU box (no\ntransformers/megatron-core/ray install and a shared container), so the two test\nfunctions were executed directly with the same `unittest.mock` patching and printed PASS:\n\n```\ntest_release_memory_delegates_to_platform PASS\ntest_resume_memory_delegates_to_platform PASS\n```\n\n### Not tested\n\n* CUDA regression — no CUDA hardware here; the equivalence argument above is the\n  substitute (`CudaPlatform.clear_memory` byte-for-byte the replaced trio).\n* End-to-end `MegatronEngine.save()` / `hf_save` execution — those call sites need a\n  real Megatron build, a checkpoint and multi-process launch; only the substituted\n  call was checked (same call, same platform object) plus the residency methods above.\n* Multi-card / distributed residency (`torchrun`), and the AWEX colocate paths, which\n  are CUDA-IPC based and out of scope.\n",
